#sec("I", "Problem Formulation and Mathematical Model")

Binary incidence matrices—encoding term presence in documents, item purchases in market baskets, gene mutations in tumor cohorts, or binarized pixel bitmaps—reside on the discrete Boolean hypercube ${0, 1}^d$. Continuous Gaussian mixtures (Algs. 93--94) ignore binary support and produce singular covariances whenever a feature is constant within a cluster, whereas Poisson mixtures (Alg. 95) leak probability mass onto counts $x_j >= 2$. *Bernoulli Mixture Models (BMMs)*—originating as dichotomous Latent Class Analysis (LCA) @lazarsfeld1968latent and standardized in modern pattern recognition via EM @dempster1977em @bishop2006pattern—model binary vectors as a finite convex combination of conditionally independent multivariate Bernoulli distributions.

Let $X = {bold(x)_1, dots, bold(x)_n} subset {0, 1}^d$. Under a $K$-component BMM with mixing weights $bold(pi) = (pi_1, dots, pi_K) in Delta_(K-1)$ and component activation probability vectors $bold(mu)_k = (mu_(k 1), dots, mu_(k d)) in (0, 1)^d$, the mixture probability mass function is
$ p(bold(x) | Theta) = sum_(k=1)^K pi_k product_(j=1)^d mu_(k j)^(x_j) (1 - mu_(k j))^(1 - x_j). $
Although coordinates $x_1, dots, x_d$ are conditionally independent given the latent cluster $Z = k$, marginalizing over $Z$ captures rich cross-feature co-occurrence patterns while keeping parameter dimensionality strictly linear at $nu = K (d + 1) - 1$.

#definition(title: "Binary KL Divergence and Log-Odds Affine Representation")[
  For $bold(x) in {0, 1}^d$ and $bold(mu) in (0, 1)^d$, define the *binary Kullback-Leibler (logistic Bregman) divergence* @banerjee2005clustering generated by $phi(bold(u)) = sum_(j=1)^d [u_j log u_j + (1 - u_j) log(1 - u_j)]$:
  $ D_"BKL" (bold(x) || bold(mu)) = sum_(j=1)^d [ x_j log(x_j / mu_j) + (1 - x_j) log((1 - x_j) / (1 - mu_j)) ]. $
  Because $x_j in {0, 1}$ implies $phi(bold(x)) = 0$ identically, each Bernoulli component log-PMF satisfies both the zero-constant Bregman identity and the log-odds affine form:
  $ log p_k (bold(x) | bold(mu)_k) = -D_"BKL" (bold(x) || bold(mu)_k) = chevron.l bold(w)_k, bold(x) chevron.r + b_k, $
  where $w_(k j) = log(mu_(k j) / (1 - mu_(k j)))$ is the log-odds vector and $b_k = sum_(j=1)^d log(1 - mu_(k j))$.
]

Parameter estimation maximizes $ell(Theta) = sum_(i=1)^n log p(bold(x)_i | Theta)$, optionally regularized by independent conjugate $"Beta"(alpha + 1, beta + 1)$ priors ($alpha, beta >= 0$) to prevent boundary collapse at $mu_(k j) in {0, 1}$, with cluster count $K$ selected by BIC ($2 ell(hat(Theta)_K) - (K d + K - 1) log n$) @schwarz1978bic.

#algbox(
  title: "Sparse-Accelerated EM for Bernoulli Mixture Models",
  inputs: [Binary matrix $X in {0, 1}^(n times d)$, cluster count $K$, optional Beta smoothing $(alpha, beta) >= (0, 0)$, tolerance $epsilon_"tol" > 0$.],
  outputs: [Mixing weights $hat(bold(pi))$, probability matrix $hat(bold(M)) in (0, 1)^(K times d)$, responsibilities $hat(tau)$, MAP partition $hat(bold(c))$.],
)[
  1. *Initialization*: Draw $bold(pi)^((0)) = (1 / K, dots, 1 / K)$ and break symmetry by initializing $mu_(k j)^((0)) ~ "Uniform"(0.25, 0.75)$ (never uniform $0.5$ across $k$) @bishop2006pattern.
  2. *E-Step (Log-Odds Affine Form)*: Compute $w_(k j)^((t)) = log(mu_(k j)^((t)) / (1 - mu_(k j)^((t))))$ and $c_k^((t)) = log pi_k^((t)) + sum_(j=1)^d log(1 - mu_(k j)^((t)))$. For each $i in [n], k in [K]$, compute
     $ eta_(i k)^((t)) = c_k^((t)) + chevron.l bold(w)_k^((t)), bold(x)_i chevron.r, $
     and normalize via $tau_(i k)^((t)) = exp(eta_(i k)^((t)) - "lse"(eta_(i 1)^((t)), dots, eta_(i K)^((t))))$.
  3. *M-Step (Closed-Form Frequencies)*: Set $n_k^((t)) = sum_(i=1)^n tau_(i k)^((t))$ and update for all $k in [K], j in [d]$:
     $ pi_k^((t+1)) = n_k^((t)) / n, quad mu_(k j)^((t+1)) = (sum_(i=1)^n tau_(i k)^((t)) x_(i j) + alpha) / (n_k^((t)) + alpha + beta). $
  4. *Convergence*: Repeat Steps 2--3 until $|ell(Theta^((t+1))) - ell(Theta^((t)))| < epsilon_"tol" (1 + |ell(Theta^((t)))|)$, then assign $hat(c)_i = arg max_k tau_(i k)^*$.
]

#sec("II", "Formal Mathematical Proofs and Theoretical Guarantees")

#subsec("A", "Exact Binary-KL Ascent Identity and Linear Decision Boundaries")

Like Poisson mixtures, BMM admits an exact information-geometric Lyapunov identity for EM while constraining every MAP cluster to a linearly separable polytope on ${0, 1}^d$.

#theorem(title: "Exact Three-Divergence EM Ascent and Linear Separability of BMM", origin: "Original Proof")[
  Let $Theta^((t)) = (bold(pi)^((t)), bold(M)^((t)))$ be any interior iterate of unregularized BMM-EM ($alpha = beta = 0$) with $n_k^((t)) > 0$ and $mu_(k j)^((t+1)) in (0, 1)$. Then:
  1. Each EM iteration satisfies the exact log-likelihood ascent identity
     $ ell(Theta^((t+1))) - ell(Theta^((t))) &= sum_(k=1)^K n_k^((t)) D_"BKL" (bold(mu)_k^((t+1)) || bold(mu)_k^((t))) \
       &quad + n D_"KL" (bold(pi)^((t+1)) || bold(pi)^((t))) \
       &quad + sum_(i=1)^n D_"KL" (tau_i^((t)) || tau_i^((t+1))) >= 0, $
     with equality iff $Theta^((t+1)) = Theta^((t))$.
  2. Every MAP cluster region $C_k = {bold(x) in {0, 1}^d : k = arg max_m tau_m (bold(x))}$ is the intersection of $K - 1$ closed half-spaces in $bb(R)^d$. Consequently, for $d >= 2$, no $2$-component BMM can separate the $d$-bit XOR (parity) partition of ${0, 1}^d$.
]

#proof[
  For part (1), using Definition 1 and letting $S_(k j) = sum_(i=1)^n tau_(i k)^((t)) x_(i j) = n_k^((t)) mu_(k j)^((t+1))$, the $(k, j)$ increment in $Q(Theta | Theta^((t)))$ is
  $ Delta Q_(k j) &= S_(k j) log(mu_(k j)^((t+1)) / mu_(k j)^((t))) \
    &quad + (n_k^((t)) - S_(k j)) log((1 - mu_(k j)^((t+1))) / (1 - mu_(k j)^((t)))) \
    &= n_k^((t)) D_"BKL" (mu_(k j)^((t+1)) || mu_(k j)^((t))). $
  Maximizing $Q$ over $mu_(k j) in (0, 1)$ has second derivative $-S_(k j) / mu_(k j)^2 - (n_k^((t)) - S_(k j)) / (1 - mu_(k j))^2 < 0$, confirming unique global optimality of $mu_(k j)^((t+1))$. Combining $sum_(k, j) Delta Q_(k j)$ with the mixing-weight gain $n D_"KL" (bold(pi)^((t+1)) || bold(pi)^((t)))$ and posterior divergence $sum_(i=1)^n D_"KL" (tau_i^((t)) || tau_i^((t+1)))$ yields the exact identity; strict positivity of $D_"BKL"$ and $D_"KL"$ ensures strict ascent unless $Theta^((t+1)) = Theta^((t))$ @dempster1977em @wu1983convergence.

  For part (2), by Definition 1, $tau_k (bold(x)) >= tau_m (bold(x))$ holds iff
  $ chevron.l bold(w)_k - bold(w)_m, bold(x) chevron.r + (c_k - c_m) >= 0, $
  where $c_k = log pi_k + b_k$. Thus $C_k$ is an intersection of $K - 1$ affine half-spaces. For $K = 2$ and $d >= 2$, fix coordinates $3, dots, d$ to $0$ and consider the 2-bit XOR face $bold(x)_"00" = (0, 0, bold(0))$, $bold(x)_"11" = (1, 1, bold(0))$ (even parity) versus $bold(x)_"10" = (1, 0, bold(0))$, $bold(x)_"01" = (0, 1, bold(0))$ (odd parity). Because $(1 / 2) bold(x)_"00" + (1 / 2) bold(x)_"11" = (1 / 2) bold(x)_"10" + (1 / 2) bold(x)_"01"$, any affine function $g(bold(x))$ satisfying $g(bold(x)_"00") > 0$ and $g(bold(x)_"11") > 0$ must satisfy $g(bold(x)_"10") + g(bold(x)_"01") > 0$, making it impossible for both odd-parity points to satisfy $g < 0$.
]

#subsec("B", "Fundamental Non-Identifiability in Low Dimensions")

Unlike Poisson mixtures (Theorem 3 of Alg. 95), finite Bernoulli mixtures suffer fundamental *non-identifiability* whenever the binary state space $2^d$ is too small to resolve all $K(d+1)-1$ parameters @bishop2006pattern @mclachlan2000finite. We prove both a sharp counting obstruction and an explicit continuum of distinct $d = 2, K = 2$ models generating the *identical* probability law.

#theorem(title: "Dimension-Counting Obstruction and Explicit Non-Identifiable Continuum", origin: "Original Counterexample")[
  1. *State-Space Obstruction*: Whenever $K > 2^d / (d + 1)$, the $K$-component BMM family on ${0, 1}^d$ is strictly non-identifiable, possessing positive-dimensional fibers of distinct parameter vectors that yield the identical joint PMF.
  2. *Explicit $d = 2, K = 2$ Continuum*: Fix any correlation $delta in (0, 1 / 4)$ and target PMF $p(1, 1) = p(0, 0) = 1 / 4 + delta$, $p(1, 0) = p(0, 1) = 1 / 4 - delta$. Every parameterization on the smooth hyperbola
     $ pi_1 = pi_2 = 1 / 2, quad bold(mu)_1 = (1 / 2 + a, 1 / 2 + b), quad bold(mu)_2 = (1 / 2 - a, 1 / 2 - b) $
     with $a, b in (2 delta, 1 / 2)$ and $a b = delta$ generates this *exact* same distribution on ${0, 1}^2$, yet distinct pairs $(a, b)$ are not label permutations of one another.
  3. *Classical Generic Identifiability* @mclachlan2000finite @lazarsfeld1968latent: For $K = 2$, generic identifiability holds if and only if $d >= 3$; more generally, Kruskal tensor-rank criteria guarantee generic identifiability when $d >= 2 ceil(log_2 K) + 1$.
]

#proof[
  For part (1), the probability simplex over ${0, 1}^d$ has dimension $2^d - 1$, whereas the parameter map $Phi: Delta_(K-1) times (0, 1)^(K d) -> Delta_(2^d - 1)$ maps a smooth manifold of dimension $K (d + 1) - 1$ into $bb(R)^(2^d - 1)$. By the rank theorem, whenever $K (d + 1) - 1 > 2^d - 1$ (equivalently $K > 2^d / (d + 1)$), every regular point has a preimage manifold of dimension at least $K(d+1) - 2^d >= 1$, precluding finite-to-one identifiability.

  For part (2), substitute the hyperbolic family directly into the 2-component BMM density for all four vertices of ${0, 1}^2$:
  $ p(1, 1) &= 1 / 2 (1 / 2 + a)(1 / 2 + b) + 1 / 2 (1 / 2 - a)(1 / 2 - b) = 1 / 4 + a b, \
    p(0, 0) &= 1 / 2 (1 / 2 - a)(1 / 2 - b) + 1 / 2 (1 / 2 + a)(1 / 2 + b) = 1 / 4 + a b, \
    p(1, 0) &= 1 / 2 (1 / 2 + a)(1 / 2 - b) + 1 / 2 (1 / 2 - a)(1 / 2 + b) = 1 / 4 - a b, \
    p(0, 1) &= 1 / 2 (1 / 2 - a)(1 / 2 + b) + 1 / 2 (1 / 2 + a)(1 / 2 - b) = 1 / 4 - a b. $
  Setting $a b = delta$ matches all four probabilities identically. Choosing $delta = 1 / 16$, both $(a, b) = (1 / 4, 1 / 4)$ and $(a, b) = (1 / 3, 3 / 16)$ lie strictly inside $(0, 1 / 2)^2$, yield non-degenerate probabilities in $(1 / 6, 5 / 6)$, and produce the exact same law despite distinct component means. For part (3), when $d = 3$ and $K = 2$, the 3-way joint probability tensor $T_(i j l) = sum_(k=1)^2 pi_k p_(k 1) (i) p_(k 2) (j) p_(k 3) (l)$ has factor matrices of full column rank $2$ whenever $mu_(1 j) != mu_(2 j)$ for all $j = 1, 2, 3$; Kruskal's uniqueness condition $2 + 2 + 2 >= 2(2) + 2$ guarantees unique recovery up to permutation @mclachlan2000finite.
]

#subsec("C", "Rank-Bounded Cross-Feature Covariance Structure")

#proposition(title: "Diagonal-Plus-Low-Rank Covariance of Bernoulli Mixtures", origin: "Original Proof")[
  Let $bold(X) in {0, 1}^d$ follow a $K$-component BMM with marginal mean $bar(bold(mu)) = sum_(k=1)^K pi_k bold(mu)_k$. Its marginal covariance matrix $bold(Sigma)_X = "Cov"(bold(X)) in bb(R)^(d times d)$ admits the exact decomposition
  $ bold(Sigma)_X = "diag"(sum_(k=1)^K pi_k mu_(k j) (1 - mu_(k j))) + sum_(k=1)^K pi_k (bold(mu)_k - bar(bold(mu)))(bold(mu)_k - bar(bold(mu)))^top. $
  In particular, the off-diagonal correlation structure of $bold(X)$ is governed by a symmetric positive-semidefinite matrix of rank at most $K - 1$. Hence any binary distribution whose off-diagonal covariance completion has rank $r$ requires at least $K >= r + 1$ components merely to match second-order moments.
]

#proof[
  Conditional on $Z = k$, the coordinates of $bold(X)$ are independent Bernoulli variables, so $"Cov"(bold(X) | Z = k) = "diag"(mu_(k 1)(1 - mu_(k 1)), dots, mu_(k d)(1 - mu_(k d)))$ and $bb(E)[bold(X) | Z = k] = bold(mu)_k$. Applying the multivariate law of total covariance,
  $ "Cov"(bold(X)) &= bb(E)["Cov"(bold(X) | Z)] + "Cov"(bb(E)[bold(X) | Z]) \
    &= "diag"(sum_(k=1)^K pi_k mu_(k j)(1 - mu_(k j))) \
    &quad + sum_(k=1)^K pi_k (bold(mu)_k - bar(bold(mu)))(bold(mu)_k - bar(bold(mu)))^top. $
  Let $bold(B)$ denote the second term. Because $bold(B) = sum_(k=1)^K pi_k bold(v)_k bold(v)_k^top$ with $bold(v)_k = bold(mu)_k - bar(bold(mu))$ satisfying $sum_(k=1)^K pi_k bold(v)_k = bold(0)$, $"span"{bold(v)_1, dots, bold(v)_K}$ has dimension at most $K - 1$, proving $"rank"(bold(B)) <= K - 1$. Since the diagonal term contributes zero off-diagonal entries, matching an off-diagonal covariance matrix of rank $r$ strictly requires $K - 1 >= r$, i.e., $K >= r + 1$.
]

#subsec("D", "Absorbing Boundary Faces and Beta-MAP Interior Regularization")

#theorem(title: "Absorbing Boundary Faces and Beta-Prior Stabilization", origin: "Original Proof")[
  1. *Global Boundedness*: For every binary dataset $X in {0, 1}^(n times d)$, $ell(Theta) <= 0$ globally (no Gaussian variance blow-up).
  2. *Absorbing Boundary Faces*: Under unregularized EM ($alpha = beta = 0$), if $mu_(k j)^((t)) = 0$ (respectively $1$) at any iteration $t$, then $tau_(i k)^((t)) = 0$ for every sample with $x_(i j) = 1$ (respectively $0$), locking $mu_(k j)^((s)) = mu_(k j)^((t))$ for all $s >= t$ and assigning probability $0$ ($ell = -infinity$) to any future test vector disagreeing on bit $j$ across all clusters.
  3. *Strict Interior Stabilization*: Adding independent conjugate $"Beta"(alpha + 1, beta + 1)$ priors with $alpha, beta > 0$ makes the MAP M-step strictly concave on $(0, 1)$ and confines every estimate uniformly inside the compact interior box
     $ mu_(k j)^((t+1)) in [ alpha / (n + alpha + beta), 1 - beta / (n + alpha + beta) ] subset (0, 1). $
]

#proof[
  Part (1) follows immediately from $0 <= p(bold(x)_i | Theta) <= 1$, hence $ell(Theta) = sum_i log p(bold(x)_i | Theta) <= 0$. For part (2), if $mu_(k j)^((t)) = 0$, the factor $(mu_(k j)^((t)))^(x_(i j)) = 0$ whenever $x_(i j) = 1$, forcing $p_k (bold(x)_i) = 0$ and $tau_(i k)^((t)) = 0$. Consequently, $sum_i tau_(i k)^((t)) x_(i j) = 0$ and $mu_(k j)^((t+1)) = 0$, persisting inductively for all $s >= t$; the symmetric argument holds at $mu_(k j)^((t)) = 1$ by replacing $x_(i j)$ with $1 - x_(i j)$. For part (3), the log-prior adds $sum_(k, j) [alpha log mu_(k j) + beta log(1 - mu_(k j))]$ to $Q(Theta | Theta^((t)))$, whose second derivative with respect to $mu_(k j)$ is $-(S_(k j) + alpha) / mu_(k j)^2 - (n_k^((t)) - S_(k j) + beta) / (1 - mu_(k j))^2 < 0$ everywhere on $(0, 1)$. Setting the first derivative to zero gives $mu_(k j)^((t+1)) = (S_(k j) + alpha) / (n_k^((t)) + alpha + beta)$. Since $0 <= S_(k j) <= n_k^((t)) <= n$, the minimum possible value is $alpha / (n + alpha + beta) > 0$ (at $S_(k j) = 0, n_k = n$) and the maximum is $(n + alpha) / (n + alpha + beta) = 1 - beta / (n + alpha + beta) < 1$ (at $S_(k j) = n_k = n$).
]

#sec("III", "Complexity, Geometry and Operational Profile")

Using the log-odds affine reformulation in Definition 1, BMM executes each E-step via a single matrix multiplication $X bold(W)^top$ plus a length-$K$ bias addition $bold(c)$, costing $cal(O)(n K d)$ operations on dense binary matrices and collapsing to $cal(O)(K ("nnz"(X) + n))$ on sparse binary incidence matrices—a dramatic speed advantage over naive dense evaluation of both $x_(i j) log mu_(k j)$ and $(1 - x_(i j)) log(1 - mu_(k j))$! Each M-step computes $tau^top X$ in $cal(O)(n K d)$ dense or $cal(O)(K "nnz"(X))$ sparse time. Over $T$ EM iterations, total complexity is $cal(O)(T n K d)$ dense and $cal(O)(T K ("nnz"(X) + n))$ sparse, with memory $cal(O)(n K + K d)$.

Geometrically, BMM partitions ${0, 1}^d$ via linear log-odds hyperplanes (Theorem 1), matching binary KL Bregman Voronoi cells @banerjee2005clustering. Crucially, unlike Gaussian models whose covariance matrices blow up in high dimensions, BMM *improves* theoretically as $d$ grows because the $2^d$ hypercube rapidly satisfies Kruskal's generic identifiability bound $d >= 2 ceil(log_2 K) + 1$ while parameter count remains strictly linear in $d$.

