#sec("V", "Structural Relations and Empirical Behavior")

BMM links statistical latent class theory @lazarsfeld1968latent, Mixmod's qualitative models (Alg. 94) @biernacki2006mixmod, binary $k$-modes @huang1998extensions, and $k$-means @lloyd1982least through an exact symmetric-noise reduction.

#proposition(title: "Exact Reduction of Symmetric BMM to Hamming k-Means and k-Modes", origin: "Original Derivation")[
  Restrict a $K$-component BMM to equal mixing weights $pi_k = 1 / K$ and Mixmod's isotropic dispersion model $[p epsilon]$ ($m_j = 2$), where each component has a binary prototype $bold(a)_k in {0, 1}^d$ and symmetric bit-flip rate $epsilon in (0, 1 / 2)$, so $mu_(k j) = 1 - epsilon$ if $a_(k j) = 1$ and $mu_(k j) = epsilon$ if $a_(k j) = 0$. Then:
  1. The negative component log-likelihood is an exact affine function of the Hamming distance $d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k) = norm(bold(x) - bold(a)_k)_1 = norm(bold(x) - bold(a)_k)_2^2$:
     $ -log p_k (bold(x)) = d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k) log((1 - epsilon) / epsilon) - d log(1 - epsilon). $
  2. In the zero-noise limit $epsilon -> 0^+$, BMM-EM converges pointwise to hard $k$-modes @huang1998extensions (equivalently binary $k$-means @lloyd1982least under squared Euclidean distance), minimizing $sum_(i=1)^n d_H (bold(x)_i, bold(a)_(c_i))$.
]

#proof[
  For any coordinate $j$, if $x_j = a_(k j)$ then $mu_(k j)^(x_j) (1 - mu_(k j))^(1 - x_j) = 1 - epsilon$; if $x_j != a_(k j)$ it equals $epsilon$. Since exactly $d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k)$ coordinates mismatch and $d - d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k)$ match,
  $ p_k (bold(x)) = epsilon^(d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k)) (1 - epsilon)^(d - d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k)). $
  Taking $-log$ yields $d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k) log((1 - epsilon) / epsilon) - d log(1 - epsilon)$, proving part (1). Notice $norm(bold(x) - bold(a)_k)_2^2 = sum_j |x_j - a_(k j)|^2 = sum_j |x_j - a_(k j)| = d_H (bold(x), bold(a)_k)$ on ${0, 1}^d$. Because $log((1 - epsilon) / epsilon) -> +infinity$ as $epsilon -> 0^+$, the E-step posterior $tau_(i k) = (1 + sum_(m != k) ((1 - epsilon) / epsilon)^(-(d_H (bold(x)_i, bold(a)_m) - d_H (bold(x)_i, bold(a)_k))))^(-1)$ converges to $bb(1){k = arg min_m d_H (bold(x)_i, bold(a)_m)}$, and the M-step majority vote $hat(a)_(k j) = bb(1){mu_(k j) > 1 / 2}$ recovers $k$-modes / binary $k$-means identically.
]

On the ClusterOverview 4-dataset empirical benchmark (Silhouette @rousseeuw1987silhouettes and ARI @hubert1985comparing), BMM's behavior strikingly confirms our theoretical low-dimensional obstruction versus high-dimensional identifiability dichotomy:
- *Iris*: $"Sil" = 0.204$, $"ARI" = 0.498$. Binarizing 4 continuous floral attributes compresses the entire dataset onto at most $2^4 = 16$ hypercube vertices, discarding fine metric separation between _Versicolor_ and _Virginica_ and yielding the lowest Iris accuracy among Algs. 93--96.
- *Customers*: $"Sil" = 0.322$. Binarized spending indicators retain macro-level customer segmentation remarkably well, virtually matching Mixmod ($0.328$) and decisively outperforming Poisson mixtures ($0.189$).
- *Multi-Shape*: $"Sil" = "N/A"$ ($"ARI" = "N/A"$). This empirical breakdown directly validates Theorem 2: binarizing 2D spatial shapes ($d = 2$) collapses every point onto at most $2^2 = 4$ binary states where $K >= 2$ BMMs are mathematically unidentifiable and frequently collapse into degenerate / trivial partitions for which Silhouette is undefined!
- *High-D*: $"Sil" = 0.303$ ($"ARI" = "N/A"$). A striking reversal: BMM achieves the *second-highest* High-D Silhouette across Algs. 93--96, outperforming both Mixmod ($0.295$) and Poisson mixtures ($0.249$) and trailing only MCLUST ($0.321$). As predicted by Theorem 2(3) and Section III, high $d$ unlocks exponential state-space capacity ($2^d$) and strict generic identifiability while preserving $cal(O)(K d)$ statistical efficiency.
- *Runtime*: Mean runtime is $3.923$ ms—second fastest after Poisson mixtures ($3.468$ ms) and $17.4 times$ faster than MCLUST ($68.368$ ms)—confirming the lightweight $cal(O)(T n K d)$ cost of log-odds binary EM.
